- Looks as if many parts of this will have to be implemented:
    http://www.yardley.cc/atari/compendium/atari-compendium-appendb.htm
    http://www.yardley.cc/atari/compendium/atari-compendium-appendb2.htm
- Mode 7 of Pexec takes the program flags a program header carries, and tosemu
  acts on none of them, so it is mode 5 with an argument it ignores. Same for
  the flags in a program header itself.
- A child of Pexec mode 0 gets the whole of memory rather than a TPA carved
  out of what its parent left free, so it is handed more than TOS would give
  it. An application that Mallocs before it Mshrinks gets nothing, where on
  TOS it would get what the parent released. The modes that load a program
  into the caller's memory do carve it out, and do not have this.
- Only sixteen children of the asynchronous Pexec modes can be waited for at
  once, and Pwait3 ignores the resource usage it is handed, which tosemu has
  nothing to fill in.
- The BIOS device handles, -1 CON:, -2 AUX: and -3 PRN:, which GEMDOS accepts
  everywhere a handle is taken. Lattice's startup code calls Fforce(4, -2).
  tosemu has a console but no serial port or printer, so -1 is the one with an
  answer to give, and the other two keep reporting EIHNDL.
- Console output goes straight to the host rather than through handle 1, so
  Cconout and Cconws ignore an Fforce that redirected it.
- Ptermres, which has nowhere to stay resident in.
- remove_memory_area never advances its pointer, so it only ever finds the
  first area. reset_memory happens to always ask for that one.
- Objects an application draws itself - G_USERDEF, which a resource editor
  calls G_PROGDEF - are drawn as empty boxes. The object's ob_spec points at
  an APPLBLK holding the address of a routine in the machine's memory, and
  calling it is more than reaching it: what the routine is handed is a
  PARMBLK describing what to draw, and in that block is the address of the
  tree. That has to be the application's own tree rather than the copy
  aestree.c makes, because the application can only read its own, and it has
  to be built in the machine's memory in the machine's layout - both blocks
  are, and the routine walks them. So this needs the Supexec trampoline in
  emuvdi/gemoblib.c to construct a PARMBLK there, fill it from the host side
  of the seam, and hand back what the routine returns. lattice/EXAMPLES/WTEST
  has two of them and is the thing to test it against.
- Add a configuration item (or command line option) to select from six 
  different graphics modes:
    1. 320x200, 4bpp (Atari ST)
    2. 640x200, 2bpp (Atari ST)
    3. 640x400, 1bpp (Atari ST)
    4. 1280x960, 1bpp (Atari TT)
    5. 640x480, 18bpp (Atari Falcon)
    6. Native screen resolution split by scaling factor, 24bpp (Best possible)
- Add a configuration item (or command line option) to select the memory size 
  from what the various Atari models (ST, TT and Falcon) offered, and then the
  maximum possible given the restrictions of the APIs.- wind_update takes no lock, and with a process per application there is
  nothing yet for it to protect: each has a screen of its own and what reaches
  the desktop are its windows, so two applications cannot reach each other's
  pixels. It was a lock because every application drew into one screen. It
  becomes one again when windows belonging to different applications overlap
  and have to be composed from a single surface rather than shown side by
  side - which is the same piece of work as the surface router, and neither is
  needed until then.
- Bridge the scrap to the desktop's own clipboard, so that copying in a GEM
  program pastes into a modern one and the other way round.

  Going out has to be watched for rather than waited for: a GEM program never
  says it copied something. scrp_write only says which directory the scrap is
  in, and the cut itself is a file appearing in it, so inotify on that
  directory is not a shortcut but the only signal there is. Watch for
  IN_CLOSE_WRITE rather than IN_MODIFY, or half a file gets read: SCRAP.TXT is
  written by an ordinary program doing ordinary writes.

  Where the code goes is decided by something less obvious. The daemon looks
  like the place - it already holds the scrap path, it is session wide, and it
  outlives any one application - but Wayland only lets a client take the
  selection in answer to something the person did, and it wants the serial of
  the input event to prove it. A daemon has no seat, no focus and no serials,
  so it cannot take the selection at all. The application that wrote the scrap
  can: it has a connection, and it has the serial of the click on Copy that
  led to the write. So the watching belongs in the application process, and
  what the daemon adds is telling the others to stop offering it.

  Coming in is easier and is not inotify at all: when the desktop offers
  something, write SCRAP.TXT into the scrap directory so that a GEM program
  pasting finds one. The work there is the conversion. Text on an ST is the
  Atari character set with CRLF between lines, and the desktop's is UTF-8 with
  LF, and the two do not agree about anything above 127. SCRAP.IMG against
  image/png is a second conversion and a larger one; text first.
- A window's frame is drawn but not worked. The sliders, arrows and size box
  appear and the elevators sit where the application says, but clicking one
  does nothing: the AES sees a press in the frame and has nowhere to send it.
  Each gadget has a message - WM_ARROWED for an arrow, WM_HSLID and WM_VSLID
  for dragging an elevator, WM_SIZED for the size box - and sending them means
  working out which gadget a point is in, which is walking the same tree
  aesframe.c builds. It should be built once and kept rather than built to
  draw and thrown away, so that finding a gadget and drawing one agree by
  construction.

- A window's information line is not drawn. It is one more object in the tree
  aesframe.c builds, and the only reason it is not there is that it is
  G_BOXTEXT and so needs a TEDINFO across the seam, where every other gadget
  needs only a colour word.
- The boxes an application drags about follow the mouse correctly but can only
  be seen where a window is showing the screen they are drawn into. A rubber
  band pulled out inside a window works; one pulled across the desktop is
  followed correctly and seen by nobody, and the same goes for graf_movebox,
  growbox and shrinkbox, which animate between two places that are usually not
  both inside a window. This is the same gap dialogs had before form_dial gave
  them windows of their own, and it closes the same way: a surface for the
  drag that lives as long as the drag does, marked so the desktop leaves it
  alone. It is the overlay surface in the plan.
- The file selector lists a directory and answers with what was chosen, and
  what it has not been watched doing is somebody picking a file out of the list
  with the mouse. Listing, walking into a folder and the drive buttons are all
  EmuTOS's and are as tested as the rest of the reused library; the part that
  is new is underneath, in emuvdi/hostfs.c, and that is exercised by the
  listing appearing at all.

- emuvdi/hostfs.c answers four of GEMDOS's calls against the host filesystem
  rather than against tosemu's own GEMDOS, because every entrance to that
  reads its arguments off the 68000's stack. It is a second implementation of
  Fsfirst and Fsnext and should not become a third of anything: what it shares
  with the original is tos_path_to_host rather than a copy of the translation.
  If more of GEMDOS is wanted from the host side - shel_find will want it - the
  answer is to give gemdosfile.c host callable entry points and have both use
  them, not to keep adding here.
